{"canonical_record":{"canonical_payload_sha256":"86b52e9684a4f9526789b1a2df29a6b5541bee4358aff7d4b86e11d772dbbba1","full_review_record_id":"fullrev_a4af02f8e56aaf2e4484b6c280a6c3cb95086a5cd741cf2d0beee00ffcb3f555","overall_assessment":"The paper presents Doc2VecC, a document representation framework that combines the simplicity of averaging word embeddings with a corruption mechanism to enforce semantic consistency and data-dependent regularization. The primary originality lies in the theoretical analysis linking the corruption process to a specific regularization term that suppresses non-discriminative words, as well as the resulting efficiency gains in both training and inference compared to methods like Paragraph Vectors.","paper_id":"B1Igu2ogg","paper_identity_sha256":"29d3de15578a900a91a6c32a39fb05eac674372d0b559216db44e0a1f8edb4fb","prompt_identity_sha256":"ce950dd4a1052234932ae093d7b74743588e2f1a4667d80a0ce6ec18562bb1a2","raw_response_sha256":"f5bba4798a36f0dfcb5cb39358d04771f0592c315db3c34d1db77fc70053a6bf","request_identity_sha256":"4340fd5be5f1245b83d4c18602e977dfbab338b87c2a2730ba3e0a0149a3d4ba","reviewer_identity_sha256":"32b29c26fbf1508e066dfb25b1a9a3fe0654c5869a7da3020e1e8e61940befe3","role":"ORIG","schema_version":"agentreview-full-review-corpus-v4","structural_context_sha256":"5437ae772fd01d90cdce9afe503559c28ca64d281d16679be3fc84e0a0b26961","subdimension_sections":[{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-004","SRC-005"],"review_text":"The paper does not introduce a fundamentally new problem or task, as document representation learning is an established area. However, it frames the problem with a specific focus on efficiency and the decoupling of model complexity from corpus size, which is a meaningful constraint not always prioritized in prior work like Paragraph Vectors. The motivation is grounded in the limitations of existing methods regarding parameter growth and test-time inference costs.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-PROB"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-011","SRC-012","SRC-013"],"review_text":"The proposed method, Doc2VecC, is a modification of the Word2Vec architecture. While the use of average word embeddings for document representation is not new (citing Huang et al., 2012), the specific integration of a corruption model during training to enforce this representation is presented as a novel algorithmic design. The method is not a minor tweak but a structural change that alters the learning objective to include a regularization term derived from the corruption process.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-MTH"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-015","SRC-016","SRC-017","SRC-018","SRC-019","SRC-020"],"review_text":"A significant contribution is the theoretical analysis in Section 3.1, which demonstrates that the corruption mechanism acts as a data-dependent regularization. The paper derives that the expected log-likelihood includes a term proportional to the variance of the corruption, which effectively penalizes embeddings of frequent but uninformative words. This analysis provides a deeper understanding of why the simple averaging approach works and offers a novel insight into the relationship between corruption and regularization in representation learning.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-ANL"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-021","SRC-023","SRC-027","SRC-031","SRC-036"],"review_text":"The experimental setup utilizes standard benchmarks (IMDB, Wikipedia, SICK) and compares against established baselines. While the datasets are not new, the evaluation protocol includes a detailed analysis of training and testing efficiency, which is a valuable addition to the standard accuracy-focused evaluations. The paper also includes a specific experiment to visualize the effect of the data-dependent regularization on word embeddings, which is a useful diagnostic not commonly seen in similar works.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-EXP"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-004","SRC-012"],"review_text":"The paper combines the existing technique of averaging word embeddings with a corruption model (similar to dropout or denoising autoencoders). The rationale for this combination is well-articulated: the corruption reduces the number of parameters updated during backpropagation (speedup) and introduces a regularization that favors informative words. This synergy between efficiency and representation quality is a compelling justification for the combination.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-COM"},{"paper_anchor_ids":[],"review_text":"The paper does not primarily focus on negative results or critical assessments of existing methods as a main contribution. While it notes that Doc2VecC performs slightly worse than LSTM-based methods on short sentences (SICK dataset), this is presented as a limitation of the method rather than a systematic failure analysis or a critical evaluation of the field. The focus remains on the positive contributions of the new framework.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-NEG"}]},"disposition":"CANONICAL_FULL_REVIEW_COMMITTED","item_commit_sha256":"08bc8f0d8771001d7d6388d0703c9cc1e88db9ecccc6a38534b78be0b8c3304f","paper_id":"B1Igu2ogg","paper_ordinal":7,"raw_attempts":[{"assistant_content_base64":"","assistant_content_sha256":"f5bba4798a36f0dfcb5cb39358d04771f0592c315db3c34d1db77fc70053a6bf","attempt_index":0,"error":"","finish_reason":"stop","paper_id":"B1Igu2ogg","raw_provider_response_base64":"","raw_provider_response_sha256":"285b18ae989dd2daf4d3146ac2e90896b7d301661637a8de246145fe574a3ae6","request_identity_sha256":"4340fd5be5f1245b83d4c18602e977dfbab338b87c2a2730ba3e0a0149a3d4ba","role":"ORIG","schema_version":"no-memory-full-review-corpus-build-v6","usage":{"completion_tokens":1015,"prompt_tokens":20098,"prompt_tokens_details":null,"total_tokens":21113},"valid":true}],"request_identity_sha256":"4340fd5be5f1245b83d4c18602e977dfbab338b87c2a2730ba3e0a0149a3d4ba","role":"ORIG","role_ordinal":3,"schema_version":"corpus-build-bootstrap-checkpoint-v4"}